}=0 \\\\ -26{x}_{1}+(-10+2\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A+2\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}(5+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}(5+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{5+\\text{i}}{-2}=-\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}.$" 13194139534741 17592186045417 true] [17592186176481 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-2\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 4 \\\\ -26 & -10\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10+2\\text{i} & 4 \\\\ -26 & -10+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(10+2\\text{i}){x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -26{x}_{1}+(-10+2\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A+2\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}(5+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}(5+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{5+\\text{i}}{-2}=-\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}.$" 13194139534741 17592186045417 false] [17592186176575 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534741 17592186045417 true] [17592186176575 1071 "Determine whether $v = \\begin{bmatrix}\\textrm{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" 13194139534741 17592186045417 false] [17592186176575 1071 "Determine whether $v=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" 13194139534741 17592186045417 true] [17592186176575 1074 "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2+2\\text{i} \\\\ 4-4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{\\text{i}}^{2}+2\\text{i} \\\\ 4-4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i}(2-2\\text{i}) \\\\ 2(2-2\\text{i})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(2-2\\text{i})[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(2-2\\text{i})v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=(2-2\\text{i})v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2-2\\text{i}.$" 13194139534741 17592186045417 true] [17592186176975 1064 "Let's find the eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-\\textrm{i}$ of the matrix ![](images/a3/a351d711bda1d0b7009e0c2aa9ac8265e061e103f24dc2ff8c221711189a57e7.svg)\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-\\textrm{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(1+\\text{i}){x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+(-1+\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-\\textrm{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another.\n\nAs a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_2 = (1+\\textrm{i})x_1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $x_1\\in\\mathbb C$ to be the free variable, we find that the general solution to our system is given by\n\n![](images/8e/8eef5a23053809341f427e7099c364d65fa24d8144e4feac2a9d817accaa5a88.svg)\n\nSetting $x_1=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n![](images/40/40c4333ba45ec2afa3479f682fcba72c2b61d69c4a4611b30b0d4078731e0a8b.svg)\n\nNotice that our eigenvector has complex-valued entries.\n\n**Note:** To check our result, we can show that $A\\mathbf v = \\lambda \\mathbf v.$ Computing $A\\mathbf v,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand computing $\\lambda \\mathbf v$ gives\n\n![](images/12/12fed2087f821e6e5da14d844b61e8300c8a4dc98ac4eff96f719fcc5795b5b4.svg)\n\nSo $A\\mathbf v = \\lambda \\mathbf v,$ as required." 13194139534741 17592186045417 true] [17592186176975 1064 "Let's find the eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-\\textrm{i}$ of the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nTo compute th